{
  "id": "732ea50c-5e22-4499-9fde-f498c5bd69a9",
  "productId": "billing",
  "title": "Bill Line Item Pricing Source",
  "updatedAt": 1793549107687,
  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "HD5aJiKd1oPVNZvd",
      "productId": "billing",
      "question": "Where in the code are bill line items priced from a stock item or billable service, and how does that pricing get applied?",
      "generation": "g-ab363e",
      "kind": "grounded",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "There are two places a bill line item gets its price, and both hinge on one shared lookup table, `CashierItemPrice` — a price row that can be attached to either a stock item (a drug/commodity in the stock module) or a billable service (a test or procedure).",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## The price table: `CashierItemPrice`",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "`CashierItemPrice` holds a `name`, a `BigDecimal price`, an optional `paymentMode`, and two optional references: `item` (a `StockItem`) and `billableService` [1]. That single table is what both pricing paths query, through `ItemPriceService`: `getItemPrice(StockItem)` for commodities [2] and `getServicePrice(BillableService)` for services [3]. Both queries sort by `id` descending, so the newest price row wins when several exist.",
          "citations": [
            1,
            2,
            3
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## Path 1 — automatic billing from a clinical order",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "When a clinician places an order, an order-billing listener dispatches it to a strategy. There are two strategies in `api/.../billing/impl`, both extending `AbstractDefaultOrderBillingStrategy`.",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "**Drug orders → stock item price.** `DrugOrderBillingStrategy` takes the drug on the order, looks up its stock items with `stockManagementService.getStockItemByDrug(drugId)`, and picks the first one [4]. Pricing is resolved by its private `resolvePrice(StockItem)`: it first asks for the item's `CashierItemPrice` rows and uses the first one's price; if there are none, it falls back to the stock item's own `purchasePrice`; if that is also null, the price is zero [5]. The line item is built with the drug order's quantity and then stamped with the stock item itself [6].",
          "citations": [
            4,
            5,
            6
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Test orders → billable service price.** `TestOrderBillingStrategy` searches for an enabled billable service whose concept UUID matches the order's concept [7]. Its `resolvePrice(BillableService)` only consults `itemPriceService.getServicePrice(...)` and returns the first row's price, or zero if there is none — there is no purchase-price fallback here because a service has no stock cost [8]. Quantity is hard-coded to 1 and the line item is stamped with the billable service [9].",
          "citations": [
            7,
            8,
            9
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## How the price is applied to the line item",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Both strategies funnel into the shared helper `createLineItem(price, quantity, status, order)` on the base class, which sets `price`, `quantity`, `status`, `lineItemOrder = 0` and the originating order on a new `BillLineItem` [10]. `BillLineItem` carries both `item` (StockItem) and `billableService` fields alongside `price`, `quantity` and `status` [11], which is why either path can fill in its own reference. The line item is attached to a new `PENDING` bill via `bill.addLineItem(lineItem)` and saved with `billService.saveBill(bill)` [12]. Each line total is `price × quantity`, computed by `BillLineItem.getTotal()` [13]. Exemptions do not change the price, only the status (`EXEMPTED` vs `PENDING`) [14].",
          "citations": [
            10,
            11,
            12,
            13,
            14
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "```mermaid\n---\ntitle: Pricing a bill line item from an order\n---\nflowchart TD\n  order[\"Clinical order\"] --> strategy{\"Order type\"}\n  strategy -->|DrugOrder| drug[\"DrugOrderBillingStrategy\"]\n  strategy -->|TestOrder| test[\"TestOrderBillingStrategy\"]\n  drug --> si[\"Stock item for the drug\"]\n  si --> ip1[\"CashierItemPrice rows for item\"]\n  ip1 -->|none| pp[\"Stock item purchasePrice\"]\n  ip1 -->|first row wins| price[\"price\"]\n  pp --> price\n  test --> bs[\"Enabled billable service for concept\"]\n  bs --> ip2[\"CashierItemPrice rows for service\"]\n  ip2 -->|first row wins| price\n  price --> li[\"createLineItem: price, quantity, status\"]\n  li --> bill[\"PENDING Bill saved\"]\n```",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## Path 2 — manual pricing from the SPA",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "When a cashier instead picks items by hand in `openmrs-esm-billing-app`, the price is chosen in the browser, not recalculated on the server. In the billing form, an item's `servicePrices` are read from the billable service; if exactly one price exists it becomes the default, otherwise the user must pick a payment mode, and `updatePaymentMethod` sets the line's `price` and `priceName` from the chosen price entry [15] and [16]. The check-in form does the same and falls back to the first service price when no price matches the selected payment method [17]. The SPA builds line items with `price`, `priceName` and `status` already filled in [18]. On the server side, `BillLineItemResource` accepts `price` and `priceName` straight from the payload [19], and the `Bill.addLineItem(item, price, priceName, quantity)` convenience method takes the price as an argument rather than deriving it [20]. Note that the `priceUuid` setter/getter in `BillLineItemResource` is dead code — `itemPrice` is always null there [21].",
          "citations": [
            15,
            16,
            17,
            18,
            19,
            20,
            21
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## What I could not verify",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "- The order-billing listener that routes orders into `DrugOrderBillingStrategy`/`TestOrderBillingStrategy` — I inferred the strategy roles from the classes and their tests, but did not read the dispatcher.\n- Whether the SPA ever sends `priceUuid` in a way the backend honours: the backend setter ignores it, so a chosen price row is not persisted as a `CashierItemPrice` link on the line item.\n- Stock managment's own `purchasePrice` semantics (that module is outside the repositories I was given).",
          "citations": []
        }
      ],
